    "updated_at": "2026-11-19T19:39:24Z",
    "body": "[@junov who's most knowledgeable of Chromium's canvas stack]\n\n@karlcow's description and probe match Chromium's implementation, with a few clarifications. This path is layered on top of our deferred-recording optimization rather than being a deliberate print model. A 2D canvas buffers draw ops and rasterizes and discards them at every flush. At print time we can replay the pending ops only if they fully describe the canvas, i.e. they start from transparent black. In practice that means clearRect/opaque drawImage covering the canvas, reset(), a resize, or a fresh canvas. \n\nOffering a mechanism for clients to opt into improved printing sounds great, and I agree with @schenney-chromium's points. An explicit signal is strongly preferred, and the suggested callback seems reasonable. Because it starts from a blank context, it captures the complete set of draw ops by construction, which is exactly what Chromium's implementation needs. We'd keep the current implicit behavior for compatibility, but I wouldn't propose standardizing it.\n\nOne caveat: some content can still knock us off the vector path in the Skia PDF backend (filters, blurs, some shaders, etc.; presumably true to some degree for all implementations). So this should be positioned as a best effort to avoid rasterization, not a guarantee,",
    "author_association": "NONE",
